A||||8||86GT-FstBk||poor highway handling, HELP||||9||
Z||000000||86GT-FstBk||07-09-2000||05:54 PM||||My 86 fastback is extremely unstable on the highway.  At any speed the rear seems to slide to follow my front end.  At 45+ mph it feels like my rear is shaking and vibrating.  Also my steering feels loose like it wants to go where it wants when I let go of the wheel.  I have replaced both shocks, struts, inner tie rods, new rear tires, all tires balanced and pressure double checked and finally a 4 wheel alignment.  I would really like to fix this problem mainly because this car is driven at highway speeds often.  TIA||||reg||9
Z||000001||PJ||07-09-2000||07:17 PM||||The rear end coming loose at high speeds could be the result of the rear wheels being toed out. The shaking and vibration could be a bent wheel or even a bad tire. Try swapping them with the fronts to confirm if its a tire/wheel assembly. Loose steering/wandering (assuming the tie rods are good, inner and outer) could be not enough caster. I know the '88's like a lot of positive caster, 4 or 5 degrees. Hope this helps.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||07-09-2000||08:14 PM||||Have you checked the ball joints??||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||07-09-2000||10:08 PM||||Mine wanted to swap ends above 25 mph when I replaced my rear tires with S-rated tires, leaving the H-rated tires on the front.  No other changes at that time, but putting the old H tires back on fixed it, and new H-rated tires of a different make didn't cause it to come back.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||07-09-2000||10:08 PM||||Maybe the rear control arm bushings?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick's Dad||07-09-2000||10:24 PM||||Hi,<P>The rear end thing sounds like wheel balance. Most other things (although I've never mixed tires, and, therefore, have no experience there) should be noticable even at low speeds. I'd still recheck the balance, even as you had it done.<P>The front end sounds to me, as well as a previous post, to be ball joints. In my experience with the uppers, it will "tighten up" temporarily when pushed, but you don't want to push it much, nor often, as this is dangerous. My most recent run in with lowers is that it just feels mousy, especially at speed.<P>Whether I'm right, or another opinion/judgement here is, this is something you should get checked right away. It could cost you as little as another set of tires (In the case of the toe out), or as much as a life (ball joints).||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||07-09-2000||11:03 PM||||Check and double check tires, wheels, ball joints (including the rear), tie rod ends (including the rear), control arm bushings, cradle bushings (a loose cradle will cause bad things), shocks, struts, and alignment.<P>Just because they did an alignment, don't mean it's right. Fieros are hard, time consuming and sometimes need a special shim kit to align correctly. It's easier for the alignment shop to just make the easy adjustments and tell you that everything else is in specs. I had a shop that tried to send me out the door without a printout of the reading once. I demanded the print out. They claimed to have ran out of printer paper. I made another appointment and the realigned it for free in order to get the printout...guess what? Even after doing the second alignment the front caster was still out of specs because they didn't have the right shims. At first they tried to tell me there was no way to adjust it, until I told them about the shims and showed them where to put the shims. They declined my offer of buying the shims and redoing the alignment for a third time at no additional cost. They decided to refund my money and let me go find another shop instead.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||07-10-2000||10:32 AM||||There is also a change to the spec's for alignment that came out in a TSB. I think Ogre had the specs and the TSB number.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||07-10-2000||04:30 PM||||Good going,Batboy,I've been lucky with alignments,but that printout is critical so you can see where you're at.I had to go back a couple times after I did the balljoint mod because the slots weren't big enough in my upper control arms.||||reg||1
